--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> SORT_DESC()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"3", "2"=t"2", "3"=t"1"}
===

### name: rel.sort.text
--- source
LIST("b", "a", "c") .> SORT()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"a", "2"=t"b", "3"=t"c"}
===

### name: rel.sort.mixed
--- source
LIST("text", 42, TRUE, NULL) .> SORT()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-, "2"=TRUE, "3"=t"42", "4"=t"text"}
===

### name: rel.sort-by.default-asc
--- source
LIST(RECORD("n", 30), RECORD("n", 10), RECORD("n", 20)) .> SORT_BY(_["n"]) .> MAP(_["n"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"10", "2"=t"20", "3"=t"30"}
===

### name: rel.sort-by.explicit-desc
--- source
LIST(RECORD("n", 30), RECORD("n", 10), RECORD("n", 20)) .> SORT_BY(_["n"], "DESC") .> MAP(_["n"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"30", "2"=t"20", "3"=t"10"}
===

### name: rel.sort-by.custom-binder
--- source
LIST(RECORD("n", 2), RECORD("n", 1)) .> SORT_BY(R, R["n"]) .> MAP(_["n"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"2"}
===

### name: rel.sort-by.custom-binder-desc
--- source
LIST(RECORD("n", 1), RECORD("n", 2)) .> SORT_BY(R, R["n"], "DESC") .> MAP(_["n"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2", "2"=t"1"}
===

### name: rel.sort-by.bad-dir
--- source
SORT_BY(LIST(1, 2), _, "UP")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG
===

### name: rel.sort-by.stable
--- source
LIST(RECORD("k", 1, "id", "first"), RECORD("k", 1, "id", "second")) .> SORT_BY(_["k"]) .> MAP(_["id"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"first", "2"=t"second"}
===

### name: rel.pipeline.query
--- source
LIST(RECORD("cat", "A", "val", 10), RECORD("cat", "B", "val", 50), RECORD("cat", "A", "val", 30), RECORD("cat", "B", "val", 20)) .> FILTER(_["cat"] $== "A") .> SORT_BY(_["val"], "DESC") .> SELECT_COLS("val")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"val"=t"30"}, "2"=-{"val"=t"10"}}
===

### name: rel.map.negation-then-keyless-sort
--- note
A keyless SORT compares the MAP's outputs, so it cannot run before the MAP.
An optimiser may move a sort ahead of a MAP only when the sort key reads
nothing but fields the MAP passes through unchanged -- never for a keyless
sort, a key that reads `_K`, or a key that reads the whole row. Every host
answered -1, -2, -3 here until this pinned it.
--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> MAP(0 - _) .> SORT()
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"-3", "2"=t"-2", "3"=t"-1"}
===

### name: rel.map.negation-then-top
--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> MAP(0 - _) .> TOP(1)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"-3"}
===

### name: rel.map.computed-record-then-sort-by-whole-row
--- note
The key reads a pass-through field and the whole row: COUNT(_) is 2 after
the MAP and 1 before it, which flips the key's sign, so the sort must stay
after the MAP.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 1), RECORD("x", 2)) .> MAP(RECORD("x", _["x"], "s", 0 - _["x"])) .> SORT_BY((COUNT(_) - 1.5) * _["x"]) .> MAP(_["x"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"2"}
===

### name: rel.map.computed-record-then-filter-on-whole-row
--- note
The same rule for a FILTER after a MAP: the predicate reads a pass-through
field, but also the whole row, whose shape the MAP changed.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 1)) .> MAP(RECORD("x", _["x"], "y", 0)) .> FILTER(_["x"] > 0 AND COUNT(_) == 2)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"x"=t"1", "y"=t"0"}}
===

### name: rel.map.computed-record-then-filter-on-key
--- note
MAP renumbers from "1"; a FILTER that reads `_K` after the MAP sees the new
keys, and must not be moved in front of it.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 1), RECORD("x", 2)) .> FILTER(_["x"] > 1) .> MAP(RECORD("x", _["x"], "y", 1)) .> FILTER(_["x"] > 0 AND _K == "1")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"x"=t"2", "y"=t"1"}}
===

### name: rel.select-cols.then-filter-on-whole-row
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 1, "z", 2)) .> SELECT_COLS("x") .> FILTER(_["x"] > 0 AND COUNT(_) == 1)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"x"=t"1"}}
===

### name: rel.select-cols.then-filter-on-key
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 1), RECORD("x", 2)) .> FILTER(_["x"] > 1) .> SELECT_COLS("x") .> FILTER(_["x"] > 0 AND _K == "1")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"x"=t"2"}}
===

### name: rel.map.pass-through-key-may-sort-early
--- note
The shape the rewrite is for: the key reads only a field the MAP passes
through, so sorting first is the same value, and what the late
materialisation check in each host's optimiser test asserts.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 2), RECORD("x", 1)) .> MAP(RECORD("x", _["x"], "y", _["x"] + 1)) .> SORT_BY(_["x"], "DESC")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"x"=t"2", "y"=t"3"}, "2"=-{"x"=t"1", "y"=t"2"}}
===

### name: rel.sort.then-filter-on-key
--- note
A sort renumbers from "1"; a FILTER that reads `_K` after it reads the
sorted position, and must not be moved in front of the sort.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 2), RECORD("x", 1)) .> SORT_BY(_["x"]) .> FILTER(_K == "1")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"x"=t"1"}}
===

### name: rel.sort.then-sort-by-key
--- note
The second sort keys on the positions the first sort assigned, so the first
sort is not redundant.
--- source
LIST(3, 1, 2) .> SORT() .> SORT_BY(_K, "DESC")
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"3", "2"=t"2", "3"=t"1"}
===

### name: rel.sort.then-sort-keeps-the-tie-order
--- note
Sorts are stable, so a sort after a sort keeps the earlier order among
equal keys: the first sort is the second's tie-breaker. An optimiser that
dropped the first as redundant answered a=2 first here in every host.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 2, "b", 1), RECORD("a", 1, "b", 1)) .> SORT_BY(_["a"]) .> SORT_BY(_["b"]) .> MAP(_["a"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=t"2"}
===

### name: rel.map.record-field-nothing-reads-is-still-evaluated
--- note
Review 2026-09-15 finding R. RECORD is strict and MAP evaluates its body for
every element (spec §6, §7.3), so a field that fails fails the program even
when nothing ever reads it. Four hosts rewrote a MAP's RECORD into a lazy
record whose unread fields were thunks, and answered 1 here where C++ -- whose
MAP clones what it collects, forcing them -- raised. A physical rewrite must
not change which errors a program raises; the rewrite is gone.
--- source
COUNT(LIST(1) .> MAP(RECORD("a", 1/0, "b", "k", "c", 3)))
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:35
===

### name: rel.map.record-field-fails-before-a-later-read-can
--- note
The same defect seen from a later step: the failing field is evaluated when
the record is built, so its error comes before the E_NO_KEY the index would
raise. Four hosts reported E_NO_KEY at 1:53.
--- source
(LIST(1) .> MAP(RECORD("a", 1/0, "b", "k", "c", 3)))[0]["b"]
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:30
===

### name: rel.map.record-field-nothing-reads-then-filter
--- note
With a FILTER after the MAP the hosts split three ways: JS, Python and Lisp
answered 1, PHP raised only because its FILTER copies the element (which
forces), C++ raised at the MAP. The position is the ABORT's argument, as it is
for any ABORT.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1), RECORD("id", 2)) .> MAP(RECORD("v", _["id"], "bad", ABORT("x"))) .> FILTER(_["v"] > 1) .> COUNT()
--- expect
error E_ABORT at 1:81
===

### name: rel.map.record-field-nothing-reads-then-sum
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1), RECORD("id", 2)) .> MAP(RECORD("v", _["id"], "bad", ABORT("x"))) .> SUM(_["v"])
--- expect
error E_ABORT at 1:81
===

### name: rel.map.then-filter-keeps-the-map-keys
--- note
Keys are part of the value (spec §7.3): MAP renumbers, FILTER keeps what it
is given, so the row that survives is "2". Every host's logical optimiser
moved this FILTER in front of the MAP -- exact for the rows, and wrong for
their keys -- so every host answered "1". The swap is only invisible when a
later step renumbers again without reading `_K`.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1), RECORD("id", 2)) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"])) .> FILTER(_["id"] > 1)
--- expect
tree -{"2"=-{"id"=t"2"}}
===

### name: rel.map.then-filter-then-map-of-key
--- note
The same pipeline read through `_K`: the key the last MAP sees is the one
the FILTER kept, which is the first MAP's numbering.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1), RECORD("id", 2)) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"])) .> FILTER(_["id"] > 1) .> MAP(_K)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2"}
===

### name: rel.map.then-filter-then-a-renumbering-step
--- note
The shape the rewrite is for: a MAP follows the FILTER and renumbers without
reading `_K`, so nothing can tell which of the two ran first.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1), RECORD("id", 2)) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["id"], "d", _["id"] * 2)) .> FILTER(_["id"] > 1) .> MAP(_["d"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"4"}
===

### name: rel.sort.then-filter-keeps-the-sorted-keys
--- note
A sort renumbers too, and the same swap moved a FILTER in front of it.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("x", 2), RECORD("x", 1)) .> SORT_BY(_["x"]) .> FILTER(_["x"] > 1)
--- expect
tree -{"2"=-{"x"=t"2"}}
===

### name: rel.select-cols.then-filter-keeps-the-keys
--- note
SELECT_COLS renumbers like MAP, and the same swap was applied to it.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1, "b", 1), RECORD("a", 2, "b", 2)) .> SELECT_COLS("a") .> FILTER(_["a"] > 1)
--- expect
tree -{"2"=-{"a"=t"2"}}
===

### name: rel.link.row-shape-named-left
--- note
The joined row of spec §7.4: the left binders (the relation's name, its
lowercase, `_1`), the right binders, then the promoted fields -- `id` is in
both elements, so neither side's is promoted. Each key appears once, and a
binder key holds the row this LINK bound: `X` is the same value as `_1`,
the element extended with its aliases (so `X` has four keys, and `X.x` is the
bare element). JS and Lisp built the row with `X`
and `x` twice, the first holding the bare element.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")); J = X .> LINK(Y, _1["cid"] == _2["id"]); LIST(INDEXES(J[1]), INDEXES(J[1]["X"]), INDEXES(J[1]["_1"]), J[1]["X"]["x"]["id"], J[1]["name"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"X", "2"=t"x", "3"=t"_1", "4"=t"Y", "5"=t"y", "6"=t"_2", "7"=t"cid", "8"=t"name"}, "2"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"cid", "3"=t"X", "4"=t"x"}, "3"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"cid", "3"=t"X", "4"=t"x"}, "4"=t"1", "5"=t"ann"}
===

### name: rel.link.row-shape-literal-left
--- note
A literal argument has no name to bind, so the row is `_1`, `_2` and the
promoted fields.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)) .> LINK(LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")), _1["cid"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(INDEXES(_))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"_1", "2"=t"_2", "3"=t"cid", "4"=t"name"}}
===

### name: rel.link.row-shape-named-right
--- note
The right side of the same rule: `Y` and `_2` are the same value, the right
element extended with `Y` and `y` holding the bare element. Python bound the
bare element on the right (four keys on the left, two on the right); the
seeded differential fuzz showed it as eleven join-output disagreements.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")); J = X .> LINK(Y, _1["cid"] == _2["id"]); LIST(INDEXES(J[1]["Y"]), INDEXES(J[1]["_2"]), J[1]["Y"]["y"]["name"], COUNT(J[1]["Y"]["y"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"name", "3"=t"Y", "4"=t"y"}, "2"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"name", "3"=t"Y", "4"=t"y"}, "3"=t"ann", "4"=t"2"}
===

### name: rel.link.row-shape-named-right-five-argument-form
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")); J = LINK(X, Y, L, R, L["cid"] == R["id"]); LIST(INDEXES(J[1]), INDEXES(J[1]["R"]), INDEXES(J[1]["_2"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"L", "2"=t"l", "3"=t"_1", "4"=t"R", "5"=t"r", "6"=t"_2", "7"=t"cid", "8"=t"name"}, "2"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"name", "3"=t"R", "4"=t"r"}, "3"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"name", "3"=t"R", "4"=t"r"}}
===

### name: rel.link.literal-binders-are-bare
--- note
An argument with no name is bound bare: neither `_1` nor `_2` is added as a
key of its own element. JS, PHP, C++ and Lisp extended the right literal
with a `_2` key holding itself (the left was already bare).
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)) .> LINK(LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")), _1["cid"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(LIST(INDEXES(_["_1"]), INDEXES(_["_2"])))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"cid"}, "2"=-{"1"=t"id", "2"=t"name"}}}
===

### name: rel.link-left.literal-right-null-row-is-bare
--- note
The unmatched row's right binder is shaped like the right elements, which for
a literal right side carry no `_2` key.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 8)) .> LINK_LEFT(LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")), _1["cid"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(_["_2"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=-, "name"=-}}
===

### name: rel.link.numeric-key-matches-as-equality-does
--- note
An equi-join on `==` pairs elements "as the comparison would compare them"
(spec §7.4), so every representation of one number is one key: trailing
fraction zeros, a negative zero, leading zeros and a number literal against a
text all pair. The hosts hashed the key from their formatted decimal (JS,
Lisp) or from whichever of the cached-decimal and parsed-text paths ran first
(PHP), so `1.50` missed `"1.5"` and a fresh PHP process joined `1.00` to
`"1.00"` only after some other comparison had parsed the text.
--- source
L = LIST(RECORD("id", 1.00), RECORD("id", 1.50), RECORD("id", "-0.0"), RECORD("id", "007"), RECORD("id", "1.00")); R = LIST(RECORD("id", "1.00"), RECORD("id", "1.5"), RECORD("id", "0"), RECORD("id", 7), RECORD("id", 1)); LINK(L, R, _1["id"] == _2["id"]) .> MAP(LIST(_["_1"]["id"], _["_2"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"1.00", "2"=t"1.00"}, "2"=-{"1"=t"1.00", "2"=t"1"}, "3"=-{"1"=t"1.50", "2"=t"1.5"}, "4"=-{"1"=t"-0.0", "2"=t"0"}, "5"=-{"1"=t"007", "2"=t"7"}, "6"=-{"1"=t"1.00", "2"=t"1.00"}, "7"=-{"1"=t"1.00", "2"=t"1"}}
===

### name: rel.link.numeric-key-repeated-run-agrees
--- note
The same join twice in one program, with an equality between the runs that
parses the text key: both runs answer the same count.
--- source
L = LIST(RECORD("id", 1.00)); R = LIST(RECORD("id", "1.00")); A = COUNT(LINK(L, R, _1["id"] == _2["id"])); E = L[1]["id"] == R[1]["id"]; B = COUNT(LINK(L, R, _1["id"] == _2["id"])); LIST(A, E, B)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1", "2"=TRUE, "3"=t"1"}
===

### name: rel.link.chained-binders-hold-this-links-rows
--- note
A second LINK carries the first's binders as nested records and binds `_1`
to the whole first row: the carried `_1` and `_2` of the first LINK do not
survive as keys, the binders of the second win. `id` is promoted now: the
first row has no scalar `id` for it to clash with.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")); Z = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "zz", "q")); J = X .> LINK(Y, _1["cid"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(Z, _1["cid"] == _2["id"]); LIST(INDEXES(J[1]), COUNT(J[1]["_1"]), COUNT(J[1]["X"]), J[1]["_2"]["zz"], J[1]["id"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"X", "2"=t"x", "3"=t"_1", "4"=t"Y", "5"=t"y", "6"=t"_2", "7"=t"Z", "8"=t"z", "9"=t"cid", "10"=t"name", "11"=t"id", "12"=t"zz"}, "2"=t"8", "3"=t"4", "4"=t"q", "5"=t"7"}
===

### name: rel.link-left.unmatched-row
--- note
An unmatched left element keeps its own binders and promoted fields; the
right binders hold a record shaped like the right elements with every field
NULL, and nothing of the right is promoted.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7), RECORD("id", 2, "cid", 8)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "ann")); J = X .> LINK_LEFT(Y, _1["cid"] == _2["id"]); LIST(INDEXES(J[2]), J[2]["_2"], J[2]["Y"], HAS(J[2], "name"))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"1"=t"X", "2"=t"x", "3"=t"_1", "4"=t"Y", "5"=t"y", "6"=t"_2", "7"=t"cid"}, "2"=-{"id"=-, "name"=-, "Y"=-, "y"=-}, "3"=-{"id"=-, "name"=-, "Y"=-, "y"=-}, "4"=FALSE}
===

### name: rel.link.predicate-reads-an-element-with-no-fields
--- note
An element with no fields is an element (spec §7.4): the predicate runs and
fails on it. C++ took a first element with nothing in it for no elements and
answered the empty list without evaluating the predicate.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1)) .> SELECT_COLS("nope") .> LINK(LIST(RECORD("id", 1)), O, C, O["x"] == C["id"])
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:83
===

### name: rel.link-left.predicate-reads-a-null-left-element
--- source
LIST(NULL) .> LINK_LEFT(LIST(RECORD("id", 1)), O, C, O["x"] == C["id"])
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:55
===

### name: rel.link-left.null-right-element-is-an-element
--- note
The right side's key expression runs over every right element first, so its
error comes before the left side's, and a NULL right element is one of them.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1)) .> LINK_LEFT(LIST(NULL), O, C, O["x"] == C["id"])
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:64
===

### name: rel.link.right-key-is-evaluated-first
--- note
The equality's right side is evaluated over the right elements before the
left side over the left ones (spec §7.4), so the missing key is reported
before the division by zero.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1)) .> LINK(LIST(RECORD("id", 1)), _1["a"] + 1/0 == _2["nope"])
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:72
===

### name: rel.link.other-predicates-run-per-pair
--- note
Not an equality of two binder reads, so it runs once per pair, left to right
within the pair.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1)) .> LINK(LIST(RECORD("id", 1)), O, C, O["a"] == C["id"] AND 1/0 == 1)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:82
===

### name: rel.link-left.no-right-elements-never-evaluates-the-predicate
--- note
No pairs, so `pred` never runs: the unmatched row is emitted without it.
Lisp evaluated it anyway and raised.
--- source
LIST(RECORD("a", 1)) .> LINK_LEFT(LIST(), O, C, 1/0 == 1) .> MAP(_["a"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"1"}
===

### name: rel.link.no-left-elements-never-evaluates-the-predicate
--- source
LIST() .> LINK_LEFT(LIST(RECORD("id", 1)), O, C, 1/0 == 1)
--- expect
tree -
===

### name: rel.top.zero-still-evaluates-the-list
--- note
Spec §7.4: a count of zero still evaluates the list. Lisp answered 0 with
LST unbound, and so for `SORT() .> TAKE(0)`, which its optimiser fuses into
this TOP.
--- source
COUNT(TOP(LST, 0))
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:11
===

### name: rel.top-by.zero-still-evaluates-the-list
--- source
COUNT(TOP_BY(LST, _, 0))
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:14
===

### name: rel.take.zero-after-a-sort-still-evaluates-the-list
--- source
LIST(1/0) .> SORT() .> TAKE(0)
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:7
===

### name: rel.link.then-filter.binder-is-not-a-name-after-the-link
--- note
The binders are scoped to the predicate (spec §7.4); after the LINK the row
carries them as keys. Every host's optimiser pushed a FILTER reading
`O["id"]` into the LINK's left side, where `O` is bound, and answered rows
for a program that raises E_UNDEF_VAR as written (the same pipeline through
a helper never took the rewrite). A read through the row, `_["O"]["id"]`,
is what names a side.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7)); X .> LINK(Y, O, C, O["cid"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(O["id"] > 1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:115
===

### name: rel.link.then-filter.relation-name-is-its-list-after-the-link
--- note
`X` after the LINK is the list it was bound to, so `X["id"]` is E_NO_KEY;
the optimiser took it for the left side and pushed the FILTER there.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7)); X .> LINK(Y, O, C, O["cid"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(X["id"] > 0)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:116
===

### name: rel.link.then-filter.through-the-left-binder-key
--- note
The read through the joined row's key is the shape the pushdown is for.
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7)); X .> LINK(Y, O, C, O["cid"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(_["O"]["id"] > 0) .> MAP(_["cid"])
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"7"}
===

### name: rel.link.then-filter.through-the-right-binder-key
--- source
X = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "cid", 7)); Y = LIST(RECORD("id", 7)); X .> LINK(Y, O, C, O["cid"] == C["id"]) .> FILTER(_["C"]["id"] > 7) .> MAP(_["cid"])
--- expect
tree -
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.pushed-conjunct-keeps-the-source-order
--- note
Spec 7.4: a FILTER after a LINK is evaluated as written. Every host's physical
optimiser moves `_["orders"]["amount"] > 2` under the join, and evaluated it
there on every order -- including order 2, whose status the first conjunct
rejects and whose amount is text -- so the program raised E_NOT_NUM where its
AND, short-circuiting, never reaches the amount. The pushed conjunct then ran
tentatively (SEL-0051); no rewrite moves it any more (SEL-0054), and the
join's run-time test of it keeps a row it raises on, so the FILTER raises in
the source's order.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", "x")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] $== "A" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"1"}}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.pushed-conjunct-raises-where-the-source-does
--- note
The same predicate with its conjuncts swapped reaches the amount of order 2
before anything rejects it, so it raises in either form -- at the amount.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", "x")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["orders"]["amount"] > 2 AND _["status"] $== "A") .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:330
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.pushed-right-conjunct-keeps-the-source-order
--- note
The right side too: `_["customers"]["credit"] > 2` was pushed under the inner
join over CUSTOMERS, where customer 7's text credit raised -- but customer 7
joins only order 2, which the status conjunct rejects first. The join now
tests it on the right rows only after the status conjunct, and keeps a right
row it raises on.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", "x")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] $== "A" AND _["customers"]["credit"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"1"}}
===
### name: rel.link-left.then-filter.pushed-conjunct-keeps-the-source-order
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", "x")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK_LEFT(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] $== "A" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"1"}}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.two-raising-conjuncts-report-the-first
--- note
Order 2's joined row raises in both conjuncts; the first in source order is
the one reported, at its own position, whichever was pushed where.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", "x")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["orders"]["amount"] > 2 AND _["customers"]["credit"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:330
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.conjunct-after-a-raising-one-is-not-tested-early
--- note
Order 2 joins customer 7, whose text credit raises in the first conjunct;
the second, on the order alone, is false there. Testing the second before the
join would drop the order and lose the raise the program as written reaches
first: a conjunct is tested early only when every conjunct before it is
tested on the same side (spec §7.4).
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["customers"]["credit"] > _["orders"]["id"] AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:331
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.right-conjunct-after-a-left-one-is-not-tested-early
--- note
Order 2's text amount raises in the first conjunct, on the order; the second,
on the customer alone, is false for customer 7, order 2's only match. Testing
it before the join would leave order 2 without a row and lose the raise.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", "x")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", 1), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["orders"]["amount"] > 2 AND _["customers"]["credit"] > 5) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:328
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.conjunct-after-the-tested-one-still-filters
--- note
The order conjunct passes on every row and is tested early; the customer
conjunct after it is what rejects order 2, and it must still be applied.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 5)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["orders"]["amount"] > 2 AND _["customers"]["name"] $== "n8") .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"1"}}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.conjunct-after-the-tested-one-raises-in-its-place
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 5)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["orders"]["amount"] > 2 AND _["customers"]["credit"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:361
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.passed-over.total-conjunct-lets-a-later-one-drop-early
--- note
The tier conjunct cannot raise -- every customer carries a text tier and no
order does -- so an implementation may test the amount before it, on the
orders; the value is what the predicate as written gives (spec §7.4).
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "tier", "Q"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"1"}}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.passed-over.a-customer-without-the-field-raises-where-written
--- note
Customer 7 has no tier: the joined row of order 2 raises E_NO_KEY in the
first conjunct, and testing the amount early would have dropped that order.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:303
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.passed-over.a-null-field-is-not-carried
--- note
A null value is not promoted onto the joined row (§7.4), so the read is
E_NO_KEY as for a missing key; the conjunct is not one that cannot raise.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "tier", NULL), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:317
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.passed-over.a-boolean-field-raises-where-written
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "tier", TRUE), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_BIN at 1:317
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.passed-over.a-text-that-is-not-a-number-raises-where-written
--- note
The numeric comparison's field must be a number on every customer; `"y"`
is text, so the conjunct can raise and nothing after it is tested early.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "credit", "y"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "credit", 3)); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["credit"] > 2 AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:318
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.passed-over.a-field-both-sides-carry-is-neither-side-s
--- note
Order 2 and its customer both carry a tier, so their joined row has none
(§7.4, pair by pair): `_["tier"]` is E_NO_KEY there. Order 1 has no tier and
takes its customer's. An implementation must not take the field for one
side's when rows of the other side carry it too.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1, "tier", "P")); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "tier", "Q"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:329
===
### name: rel.link-left.then-filter.passed-over.the-null-extended-side-can-raise
--- note
Order 2 has no customer: its null-extended row has no tier, and the first
conjunct raises there; under LINK_LEFT the right side's field is never one
that cannot raise.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ORDERS .> LINK_LEFT(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["orders"]["id"]))
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:277
===
### name: rel.link.chain.then-filter.nested-read-tested-on-the-base-rows
--- note
`_["orders"]["amount"]` after two joins reads the orders row nested in the
joined row -- on the base rows themselves, the row; an implementation may
test it there, behind the status and tier conjuncts that cannot raise.
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "tier", "Q"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ITEMS = LIST(RECORD("order_id", 1, "sku", "s1"), RECORD("order_id", 2, "sku", "s2")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(ITEMS, _["orders"]["id"] == _2["order_id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] $== "A" AND _["tier"] $== "P" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > 2) .> MAP(RECORD("sku", _["sku"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"sku"=t"s1"}}
===
### name: rel.link.chain.then-filter.nested-read-raises-in-its-place
--- source
ORDERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "customer_id", 8, "status", "A", "amount", 5), RECORD("id", 2, "customer_id", 7, "status", "B", "amount", 1)); CUSTOMERS = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "name", "n7", "tier", "Q"), RECORD("id", 8, "name", "n8", "tier", "P")); ITEMS = LIST(RECORD("order_id", 1, "sku", "s1"), RECORD("order_id", 2, "sku", "s2")); ORDERS .> LINK(CUSTOMERS, _1["customer_id"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(ITEMS, _["orders"]["id"] == _2["order_id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] $== "B" AND _["tier"] $== "Q" AND _["orders"]["amount"] > "x") .> MAP(RECORD("sku", _["sku"]))
--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:523
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.early.a-left-field-an-element-lacks-raises-on-its-row
--- note
The first order has no status, so neither has its joined row (§7.4 promotes
the left element's own fields, pair by pair): `_["status"]` is E_NO_KEY on the
first row. Testing the conjunct on the orders keeps that order -- the read
raises there too -- and the error surfaces as written.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "bid", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "bid", 2, "status", 0)); B = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "name", "n")); A .> LINK(B, _1["bid"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] >= 2) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:165
===
### name: rel.link.chain.then-filter.early.an-upper-join-key-still-raises
--- note
The second join computes `L["A"]["cid"]` for every row it receives, and order
2 has no cid: E_NO_KEY there, before the FILTER runs. Testing the status
conjunct on the orders would drop both and lose that error.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "bid", 2, "cid", 7, "status", 0), RECORD("id", 2, "bid", 2, "status", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "name", "n")); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "sku", "s")); A .> LINK(B, _1["bid"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["A"]["cid"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] == 5) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:239
===
### name: rel.link.chain.then-filter.early.tested-on-the-base-rows
--- note
The same shape where every key the upper join reads is present: the status
conjunct may be tested on the orders, and the answer is the one row.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "bid", 2, "cid", 7, "status", 0), RECORD("id", 2, "bid", 2, "cid", 7, "status", 5)); B = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "name", "n")); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "sku", "s")); A .> LINK(B, _1["bid"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["A"]["cid"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] == 5) .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["A"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"2"}}
===
### name: rel.link.chain.then-filter.early.a-pushed-conjunct-kept-on-error-stops-the-rest
--- note
Under explicit binders `_["C"]` is no member of the joined row: E_NO_KEY on
every row. A conjunct an implementation tests early on C's own rows, where
it raised, must not let the status conjunct after it drop the orders.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "bid", 2, "cid", 7, "status", 0)); B = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "name", "n")); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 7, "sku", "s")); A .> LINK(B, _1["bid"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["A"]["cid"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["C"]["amt"] $== "y" AND _["status"] $< "0") .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:230
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.early.under-an-explicit-binder-underscore-is-not-the-row
--- note
With FILTER(r, ...) the element is `r`; `_` is an unbound name, E_UNDEF_VAR,
whatever the rows hold.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "bid", 2, "status", 0)); B = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "name", "n")); A .> LINK(B, _1["bid"] == _2["id"]) .> FILTER(r, _["status"] == 5) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:140
===
### name: rel.link.chain.then-filter.early.a-left-field-reaches-through-two-joins
--- note
Order 2's tier is promoted by the first join (its customer has none) and again
by the second (C has none), pair by pair: the row answers, whatever order 1
lacks.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "bid", 2, "status", 0), RECORD("id", 2, "bid", 2, "status", 5, "tier", "P")); B = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "name", "n")); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 2, "sku", "s")); A .> LINK(B, _1["bid"] == _2["id"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["A"]["bid"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["status"] == 5 AND _["tier"] $== "P") .> MAP(RECORD("id", _["A"]["id"]))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=-{"id"=t"2"}}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.renumbered.a-left-conjunct-keeps-the-joined-row-keys
--- note
A FILTER after a LINK keeps the joined rows' keys (spec 7.3). The physical
optimiser moved `_["A"]["amt"] == 3` onto A before the join, so the kept row
was the join's first, not its second, and `_K` answered "1" (SEL-0054).
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1, "amt", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "b", 1, "amt", 3)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["A"]["amt"] == 3) .> MAP(_K)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2"}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.renumbered.the-result-carries-the-joined-row-keys
--- note
The same, observed in the FILTER's own result.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1, "amt", 2), RECORD("id", 2, "b", 1, "amt", 3)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); J = A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["A"]["amt"] == 3); INDEXES(J)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2"}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.renumbered.a-right-conjunct-keeps-the-joined-row-keys
--- note
A conjunct on the right side, moved onto B, renumbered the joined rows the
same way.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1, "t", 1), RECORD("bid", 1, "t", 2)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["B"]["t"] == 2) .> MAP(_K)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2"}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.renumbered.link-left-keeps-the-joined-row-keys
--- note
LINK_LEFT: its left side was filtered before the join all the same.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1), RECORD("id", 2, "b", 3)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); A .> LINK_LEFT(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["A"]["b"] > 2) .> MAP(_K)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"2"}
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.join-key.a-left-key-raises-though-the-right-rows-all-fail
--- note
The join computes its left key for every left row that has a right row to
meet; moved onto B, the conjunct emptied the right side, the join returned
before reading any left key, and A's E_NO_KEY in `_1["b"]` was lost.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1, "t", 5)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["B"]["t"] < 2)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:79
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.join-key.a-right-key-raises-though-the-left-rows-all-fail
--- note
The mirror: moved onto A, the conjunct emptied the left side, and B's E_NO_KEY
in `_2["bid"]` was lost.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("x", 1)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["A"]["id"] == 5)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:88
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.join-key.a-dropped-left-row-still-raises-in-the-key
--- note
Row 2 has no `b`: the join raises reading its key, before any FILTER sees a
row. Moved onto A, the conjunct dropped row 2 first.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1), RECORD("id", 2)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["A"]["id"] == 1) .> MAP(_K)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:96
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.join-key.an-upper-join-key-still-raises
--- note
The joined rows of A and B have no `cid`, so the second join raises reading
`L["cid"]`; the conjunct, moved below both joins, dropped every row before the
second join read a key.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1, "x", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["cid"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["a"]["x"] > 4)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:152
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.binders.an-explicit-binder-is-the-row-s-member-not-the-relation
--- note
Under explicit binders the joined row's right member is `R`; `_["C"]` is no
key of it, so the FILTER raises. The optimiser took the relation's name for
the right side and moved the conjunct onto C.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 3)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["b"] == R["id"] - 2) .> FILTER(_["C"]["id"] == 3) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:177
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.binders.a-lower-relation-s-field-is-not-the-joined-row-s
--- note
`_["A"]["cid"]` reads A's element, which has no `cid`. The optimiser rewrote
it to `_["cid"]` over the rows of the join below -- B's `cid` there -- and
dropped the row on 9 == 1.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1, "cid", 9)); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 1)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["id"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["A"]["cid"] == 1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:189
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.chain.a-middle-filter-reads-its-own-rows
--- note
The middle FILTER reads the rows of the join below it, which have no `sku`:
it raises there. The run-time pre-filter judged its conjunct against every
join up to the last FILTER, found `sku` on every row of C above, and passed it
over as unable to raise -- so the last FILTER's `x == 2`, handed down, dropped
the row first (SEL-0054). Each FILTER's conjuncts are judged against the
joins between it and the join testing them, and no others.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1, "x", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); C = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "sku", 5)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> FILTER(_["sku"] != 2) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["A"]["id"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["x"] == 2) .> MAP(1)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:155
===
### name: rel.link.then-filter.as-written.chain.an-upper-join-computes-its-right-keys
--- note
As written, the rows of A and B reach the second join, which computes C's
keys and raises: C's row has no `id`. A conjunct handed down to the first
join drops every row there; the second join, left with none, must still
compute what it would have (spec 7.4), so it runs as written on its left
side as written.
--- source
A = LIST(RECORD("id", 1, "b", 1, "x", 1)); B = LIST(RECORD("bid", 1)); C = LIST(RECORD("sku", 1)); A .> LINK(B, _1["b"] == _2["bid"]) .> LINK(C, L, R, L["A"]["id"] == R["id"]) .> FILTER(_["x"] == 2)
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:169
===
